  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nLatest GOES night fog reveals that there is very little in the way \nof any marine layer present over the inner waters, with mostly just \npatchy areas of low clouds along portions of the coastal areas of \nSan Diego County. There are also some passing mid to high level \nclouds funneling up from the transverse banding associated with \nHurricane Marie, which is located roughly around 800 miles to the \nsouth-southwest of the region. This will continue to track towards \nthe northwest and some of the moisture will become advected up ahead \nof a longwave trough upstream and with some forcing, will result in \nsome developing bands of precipitation, which given where the best \nconvergence aloft is located, has the best chance over Riverside and \nSan Bernardino Mountains, tapering to lesser amounts to the south of \nSan Diego County. Given the timing and position of where these bands \nof light to moderate rain establish themselves in a southwest to \nnortheast orientation, the better chance of rain looks to be during \nthe morning hours of Sunday with the highest PWAT values overhead.\nTotal rainfall amounts may reach over a half an inch over the San\nBernardino Mountains, and generally 0.20 to 0.30 inches within \nthe IE, with slightly lesser amounts to the coastal areas of \nOrange County, and drastically tapering off to lesser amounts over\nSan Diego County (with possibly some areas over southern San \nDiego County receiving little to no precip). These bands of rain \nwill eventually dissipate by later in the evening on Sunday. \nTemperatures will remain on the cooler side, around and slightly \nbelow the seasonal average for this time of year, and especially \nfor tomorrow as extensive cloud cover increases over the region.\n\nMonday through Friday...\n\nDrier conditions will return by Monday, with most of the moisture \nassociated with Hurricane Marie exiting the region, and only some \nlingering moisture being advected up some the south which may allow \nfor a slight chance of a afternoon shower or storm to develop over \nthe mountains. As the ridge continues to build into the middle of \nthe week, with will warm temperatures back above the seasonal \naverage, with the warmest day peaking on Wednesday, which will find \nhigh temperatures returning back into the low to mid 90s for the \ninland areas. There will still be moisture in place that will move \nup clockwise around the high pressure centered over the Four Corners \narea that will keep a slight chance of afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms for the mountains and adjacent deserts, along with a \ngradual cooldown throughout the rest of the work week. The marine \nlayer will likely also continue to be displaced and patchy \nthroughout this next week.",
